bout 10^-3 per AU, is roughly ten times larger than a spike comet's entire binding energy of about 10^-4 per AU, so on its first return a comet is usually ejected or thrown to much larger 1/a, leaving the spike forever after one visit", "Because the galactic tide steadily nudges all comets toward exactly 1/a = 0 and holds them there indefinitely", "Because comets in the spike are on stable resonant orbits with Jupiter that trap them permanently at low 1/a", "Because only comets that formed in place at tens of thousands of AU can reach such low binding energies, and there are very few of them"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The narrowness of the spike is the signature of a population each comet visits essentially once. A spike comet's binding energy corresponds to 1/a of about 10^-4 per AU, but the random energy kick Jupiter delivers on one perihelion passage is about 10^-3 per AU, roughly ten times larger than the comet's entire binding energy. A perturbation bigger than the quantity it perturbs is decisive: on the first return the comet is usually either ejected onto a hyperbola (1/a below zero) or thrown onto a much more tightly bound orbit with far larger 1/a. Either way it leaves the spike. So the comets in the spike are overwhelmingly making their first-ever passage, arriving fresh from the reservoir at their true reservoir energy, with every later apparition smearing them across larger 1/a. The narrowness is not small-number noise; it is the fingerprint of single-visit, dynamically new comets."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The long-period comets arrive with inclinations distributed as sin(i) rather than clustered near the ecliptic. What does this establish, and by what geometric reasoning?", "options": ["It establishes that the comets formed in the same flattened protoplanetary disc as the planets, because a sin(i) distribution is what a thin disc produces", "It establishes that the source is spherically symmetric: a flattened disc would launch bodies preferentially near the ecliptic and cluster them toward low inclination, so isotropic arrivals from every direction, as many retrograde as prograde, can only come from a shell surrounding the Sun on all sides", "It establishes only that the comets have been scattered by Jupiter, which randomizes their inclinations during each perihelion passage", "It establishes that the reservoir must be very massive, since only a massive disc can deflect comets to high inclinations"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A body's orbital plane must contain the point from which it was launched, so a source confined to a thin disc can only tilt orbits as far as the disc's own thickness allows; its children inherit low inclinations and cluster toward the ecliptic, exactly as the planets, asteroids, Kuiper belt, and Jupiter-family comets do, all descended from the flat natal disc. The long-period comets instead arrive isotropically, with orbital poles pointing uniformly over the sky and as many retrograde as prograde, giving a distribution proportional to sin(i), the signature of directions drawn at random from a sphere. A disc cannot aim things straight over the poles, so isotropy rules a disc out and forces a spherically symmetric source. This is the second pillar: the energies fix the size (tens of thousands of AU), the inclinations fix the shape (a spherical shell). Two orbital elements do the entire job."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter's central and most-often-misread point is that the Oort cloud did not form where it sits. What is the correct account of how its bodies got there?", "options": ["They condensed in place from the outer solar nebula at tens of thousands of AU and have remained pristine and undisturbed ever since", "They are captured interstellar objects that the Sun's gravity gathered from passing molecular clouds over billions of years", "They were blown outward by the young Sun's radiation pressure and solar wind during its early luminous phase", "They are ordinary icy planetesimals scattered outward from the giant-planet region, then had their perihelia LIFTED beyond planetary reach by galactic tides and passing stars, detaching them into a dynamical structure rather than a birthplace"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The protoplanetary disc did not extend to tens of thousands of AU; the density of material there was effectively nil and orbital times are millions of years, so nothing could accrete in place within the age of the universe, let alone the few-million-year gas-disc lifetime. The Oort cloud is not a birthplace but a place things were put. Its bodies are ordinary icy planetesimals from the giant-planet region, flung outward by gravitational kicks from Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune, especially during the migration and instability of the Nice-model era. But a scattered body with perihelion still near 30 AU is only on a leash: it re-enters the planetary shredder and is usually ejected. To store it, external forces (the galactic tide and passing stars) must torque its orbit near aphelion and lift its perihelion above roughly 45 AU, out of Neptune's reach, detaching it. The cloud is therefore a dynamical structure defined by where its ordinary contents were parked and by which forces, not a pristine primordial nursery; the popular image gets the story backwards, since it is one of the most processed structures in the solar system."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Scholz's star passed within about 52,000 AU of the Sun roughly 70,000 years ago, the closest known recent stellar passage, yet its dynamical effect was slight. Why did 'closest' not make it the most disruptive?", "options": ["Because the impulse a passing star delivers scales as its mass divided by the product of its speed and its miss distance, and Scholz's star, though close, was light (about 0.15 solar masses) and fast (about 83 km/s), so its net kick was only a small fraction of a metre per second", "Because at 52,000 AU it was still outside the outer Oort cloud entirely and so passed no comets at all", "Because the galactic tide cancelled the star's pull almost exactly during the encounter", "Because the Hills cloud shielded the outer cloud from the star's gravity during the passage"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The impulse a passing star gives a comet at closest approach scales roughly as delta-v ~ 2GM/(v_rel times b), so mass, slowness, and closeness all matter, and closeness is only one of the three factors. Scholz's star, a red dwarf of about 0.1 to 0.15 solar masses with a brown-dwarf companion (WISE 0720-0846), threaded the outer cloud at about 52,000 AU (0.25 pc), well inside it, but it won only on miss distance. It was light, about a tenth of a solar mass rather than a full Sun, cutting the kick by a factor of several, and it was fast, crossing at about 83 km/s, cutting it further versus a typical 20 km/s. The net kick to comets in its path was a small fraction of a metre per second. A hypothetical 1-solar-mass star at the same distance but 20 km/s would deliver a kick tens of times larger. Proximity alone does not measure influence; the product of mass, slowness, and closeness does."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The Oort cloud has two nested parts and a deeply uncertain census. Describe the structure of the outer cloud versus the inner Hills cloud (extent, shape, and why each has the shape it does), explain the role the Hills cloud plays as a replenishment tank, and state what is known and not known about the population and mass. Tie the differing shapes back to the same physics that governs comet injection.", "rubric": "A strong answer distinguishes the two components. The OUTER cloud is the classical Oort cloud Oort himself deduced, a roughly spherical shell from about 20,000 AU out to somewhere between 100,000 and 200,000 AU; it is spherical because the external forces (chiefly the galactic tide) act efficiently at these enormous distances and have, over billions of years, fully isotropized its orbits, erasing the memory of any original plane. It is the outer cloud that supplies most observed new long-period comets, precisely because the gentle external torques drop perihelia into view far more effectively far out. The INNER cloud, or Hills cloud (Jack Hills, 1981), runs from roughly 2,000 AU to about 20,000 AU; it is more tightly bound, so external forces disturb it less, and for that same reason it has NOT been isotropized, retaining a flattened, disc-like or toroidal shape, a fossil of the plane it was scattered from. The key link: the same tidal physics that isotropizes the outer cloud (strong torque at large a) is too weak in the tightly bound inner cloud (weak torque at small a) to stir it into a sphere, so shape correlates with binding and with injection efficiency. The Hills cloud acts as a REPLENISHMENT TANK: it is probably far more populous and, being resilient, survives stellar passages and tidal stripping that deplete the outer cloud, slowly refilling the outer regions from below; without it the outer cloud would long ago have run dry, so the continued arrival of fresh comets after 4.5 billion years is itself indirect evidence for a protected inner core. On the CENSUS: population of order 10^12 objects larger than about a kilometre (a trillion), obtained by a flux argument (a few new comets per year, each consumed and needing replacement, run back over the age of the solar system and corrected for delivery efficiency), with an order-of-magnitude uncertainty either way; total MASS known only to within a factor of ten, currently a few Earth masses (plausibly under one to a few tens), the uncertainty arising because mass depends on the unmeasurable size distribution (a few large bodies could outweigh a trillion small ones). A strong answer frames this candour as intellectual honesty when direct data are permanently out of reach, not a failing."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter makes methodology a through-line: we are justifiably certain of a structure no one has seen or ever will see. Lay out the full chain of inference from measured comet orbits to a distant, spherical, bound, scattered-and-detached reservoir, and explain what makes this inference count as knowledge rather than a placeholder awaiting a photograph. In your answer, address why resting on a DISTRIBUTION rather than a single observation strengthens the case, and why the theory being PRODUCTIVE matters.", "rubric": "A strong answer reconstructs the chain link by link. (1) Reconstructed 'original' long-period comet orbits, cleaned of planetary perturbations, pile up sharply at 1/a near zero but positive (between about 0 and 10^-4 per AU): the comets are BOUND to the Sun and come from tens of thousands of AU, not 40 AU and not interstellar space. (2) The narrowness of the spike (Jupiter's ~10^-3 per AU kick exceeds the ~10^-4 per AU binding energy) shows these are dynamically new, first-time comets carrying untouched reservoir energy. (3) Their inclinations are isotropic (distributed as sin(i), as many retrograde as prograde), so the source is a spherically symmetric SHELL, not a disc. (4) The shell cannot have formed in place (no material at tens of thousands of AU), so its ordinary icy planetesimals must have been SCATTERED from the giant-planet region and had their perihelia LIFTED beyond planetary reach by galactic tides and passing stars, detaching them. Distant, spherical, bound, scattered-and-detached: each link is forced by a distribution, not a single datum. On EPISTEMOLOGY, a strong answer explains that the argument rests not on one decisive image but on the SHAPE of two distributions (the pile-up in 1/a, the spread in sin(i)); distributions are harder to fake and misread than single measurements. A single near-parabolic isotropic comet could be a fluke, an interstellar visitor, or a measurement error; thousands piling against 1/a = 0 and spreading isotropically cannot all be flukes. The signal lives in the statistics, the same logic that reads a planet's interior from its gravity harmonics or a star's composition from spectral-line ratios. It should also stress that the inference is PRODUCTIVE, the real test: from the bare deduction dynamicists derived perihelion-lifting, which predicted the inner Hills cloud, a faint galactic-tidal fingerprint in comet arrival directions, an outer edge at the Sun's tidal radius, and occasional comet showers from close stellar passages (Scholz's star as illustration), all borne out as the catalogue grew from Oort's two dozen to thousands. A hypothesis that only fit the original data would be a curiosity; one that keeps predicting new data correctly is knowledge. The best answers conclude that this is not a promissory note awaiting a photograph but knowledge itself, differently grounded (in distributions rather than pictures) than, but not weaker than, the imaged Kuiper belt, while remaining honest about the factor-of-ten mass uncertainty."}]$astroOort_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/oort-cloud', lvl, q.questions, true
